 "cards": [
  {
   "key": "whats_going_on",
   "title": "What's going on with Sandhoff disease",
   "text": "Sandhoff disease is caused by changes in a gene called HEXB, according to HPO [E2a2538d]. A paper reports that Sandhoff disease happens when a child has two faulty copies of the HEXB gene, found through genetic testing [E4dd43d8]. A paper reports that this gene problem leads to a missing or very low amount of an enzyme called beta-hexosaminidase, which the body normally uses to break down certain fatty substances [E113e89e]. A paper reports that without enough of this enzyme, a fatty substance called GM2 ganglioside builds up inside cells, especially nerve cells [E1db4d37]. A paper reports that this buildup causes the brain's clean-up cells, called microglia, to become more active, which is linked to inflammation in the brain [Edaa04b4]. A paper reports that these changes are connected to a loss of nerve cells over time [E6723aaa].",
   "citations": [
