  {
   "key": "similar",
   "title": "Diseases that share the same biology",
   "text": "Tay-Sachs disease is biologically similar to Sandhoff disease because a paper reports that both cause the same fatty substance, GM2 ganglioside, to build up in nerve cells [E279a08b, E0d0eb92]. A paper reports that both diseases also come from the same kind of missing enzyme problem inside lysosomes, the cell's recycling centers [E57897fb, E87925e8]. According to HPO, both diseases can involve a cherry-red spot in the eye and blindness [E6e4df5e, E933ccf3, E6b86d4b, E9e9a288]. GM2 AB variant is also biologically similar to Sandhoff disease because a paper reports it involves the same kind of GM2 ganglioside buildup [E279a08b, E879b3c8]. Krabbe disease is biologically similar to Sandhoff disease because a paper reports both involve the same kind of missing-enzyme problem inside lysosomes and brain inflammation [E57897fb, E60744b8, Edaa04b4, E5aaac6b]. According to HPO, Krabbe and Sandhoff can both show developmental regression and symptoms starting in infancy [Ea716072, Ea9f4b27, E9455794, E5f8daab].",
